      "body": "Version 1.9.0 - Remove links from subtitles\n\nNew opt-in option: **Settings → Processing → \"Remove Links from Subtitles\"**\n(off by default). Implements #14.\n\nWhen enabled, dropped subtitles are scanned for:\n\n- URLs — `http(s)://`, `ftp://`, `magnet:`, bare domains, `www.` hosts\n- Email addresses\n- IPv4 addresses, with optional port and path\n- Obfuscated links — `example (dot) com`, `example [.] com`,\n  `www dot example dot com`, `hxxp://`\n- Social handles — `@releasegroup`\n\nNothing is changed silently. A preview dialog lists every match with its\nline number and category, shows a live before/after diff, and lets each\nmatch be unchecked individually before anything is applied.\n\nMatching is conservative, because subtitles are not clean prose:\n\n- A dotted token counts as a domain only when its last label is a real\n  delegated TLD, so `example.com` is stripped while `example.commute`\n  is left alone.\n- 107 TLDs are also common sentence-starting English words (`.it`, `.to`,\n  `.me`, `.now`, `.one`). Subtitles frequently drop the space after a\n  period, so those need a scheme, a `www.` prefix, or a path/port before\n  they count as links — `\"Stop.It's over\"` and `\"I know.Now go\"` survive,\n  `http://stop.it` and `stop.it/freesubs` do not.\n- A Title-Cased TLD is treated as a sentence boundary; ALL-CAPS still\n  matches, so `VISIT EXAMPLE.COM` is caught.\n\nSubtitle structure is preserved. Timing lines, indexes, ASS headers and\nMicroDVD frame markers are never touched. Cues left holding nothing but a\nlink are dropped and the remaining ones renumbered. Edits are applied over\na byte-preserving latin1 view, so cp1250/cp1251/Big5 files keep their\noriginal encoding byte-for-byte.\n\nThe TLD list is generated from IANA via `npm run generate-tlds`.\nCovered by 99 new unit tests.",
